WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.186
A linear equation connects two coordinates through a constant rate

00:00:04.186 --> 00:00:11.759
Here the output begins at one when the input is zero, then grows by two for each additional input unit

00:00:11.759 --> 00:00:15.167
Every point on this straight line satisfies the equation.

00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:22.067
Moving all variable terms to one side creates another form of the same equation

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:26.867
Subtract y and subtract one from both sides of the original equality

00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:32.067
No points are gained or lost, so the graph stays exactly the same.

00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:38.715
If the output is fixed at seven, the equation becomes a one-variable problem

00:00:38.715 --> 00:00:42.830
Subtract one and divide by two to get input three

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:47.767
Geometrically, we are finding where our line meets the horizontal level seven.

00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:53.064
Some linear equations do not depend on both coordinates

00:00:53.064 --> 00:00:59.282
Two y equals six gives the horizontal line y equals three, allowing every x

00:00:59.282 --> 00:01:05.500
A linear equation describes all satisfying pairs unless another condition selects a particular point.
